@title Argument for the Institutions Pattern Domain (Ostrom's design principles)
@keywords institutions, Ostrom, commons, common-pool resources, IAD, design principles
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone analytic-foundational
@style argument
@references [institutions/user-boundaries institutions/resource-boundaries institutions/congruence-with-local-conditions institutions/appropriation-and-provision institutions/collective-choice-arrangements institutions/monitoring-users institutions/monitoring-the-resource institutions/graduated-sanctions institutions/conflict-resolution-mechanisms institutions/minimal-recognition-of-rights-to-organise institutions/nested-enterprises institutions/rules-as-institutional-statements creativity-commons/ARGUMENT]
@provenance Ostrom 1990 (Governing the Commons); principle wording from the meta-review of Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010) as quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1; IAD and the grammar of institutions from Ostrom 2009 and 2010 as cited there. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against the literature
@summary Ostrom's design principles for long-enduring institutions that
manage common-pool resources, one pattern each, in the eleven-item form
of Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), plus Ostrom's grammar of institutional
statements. This family stays with Ostrom's own domain. The
transposition to a creativity commons is the parallel family
creativity-commons/.

! conclusion:
  Groups of users can govern a common-pool resource without the tragedy
  of the commons when their institutions have the features the design
  principles name: clear boundaries, rules fitted to local conditions,
  collective choice, accountable monitoring, graduated sanctions, cheap
  conflict resolution, recognised rights to organise, and nesting.

  + context:
    A common-pool resource is rivalrous (one party's use precludes
    another's) and nonexcludable (blocking access is hard); fisheries
    and forests are the standard examples. Ostrom's empirical work
    showed that the tragic outcome does not always transpire, and
    emphasised "the importance of group attributes and institutional
    arrangements" and "the likelihood of a broader set of possible
    outcomes, including user-group institutional solutions" (McCay and
    Acheson 1990, p. 23). An institution is the "shared concepts used by
    humans in repetitive situations organized by rules, norms, and
    strategies" (Ostrom 2010). IAD studies action situations in three
    phases: context, action, outcome.

  + IF:
    A group shares a resource that its members can deplete.

  + HOWEVER:
    The principles summarise conditions found in long-enduring cases;
    they are not a recipe, and they work together rather than one at a
    time. The non-quoted text in this family is a first draft and has
    not been checked against Ostrom 1990 or Cox et al. 2010.

  + THEN:
    Use the eleven patterns to analyse or design the institution, and
    write its rules in the form of rules-as-institutional-statements so
    they can be checked.

  + BECAUSE:
    The principles together support institutions that maintain the
    integrity of the commons, "for example, by ensuring that behaviour
    is monitored, that knowledgeable and concerned parties are the ones
    who make specific rules, and that conflicts do not get out of hand"
    (Ostrom et al. 2012, p. 79, as paraphrased in Corneli 2016).

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet each pattern's HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010, and record page references.]
    next[Related: equity/ draws on Ostrom for the UKRN evaluation; workshop/IAD-ADAPTER.md separates pattern, institution definition and participation record.]
